Lenses Bug Report: src/henchman/cli/textual_mixins.py\n## Date: 
2025-03-17T22:15:00Z\n## Bug Summary\nThe `_textual_confirm` method uses 
`id(future)` as dictionary keys for tracking confirmation futures, which can 
lead to key collisions when memory addresses are reused after garbage 
collection.\n\n## Lens\n**Phase 1: The Intent vs. Reality Check** - Logic 
Gaps\n\n## Expected vs. Observed\n| Expected Behavior | Observed Risk 
|\n|---|---|\n| Each confirmation request should have a unique identifier that 
persists for the lifetime of the confirmation dialog | Using `id(future)` 
creates identifiers that can be reused when Python recycles memory addresses 
|\n| The system should reliably track pending confirmations and clean them up 
properly | Memory address reuse can cause late callbacks to affect wrong futures
or cleanup to remove wrong entries |\n| Concurrent confirmation requests should 
not interfere with each other | Key collisions could cause race conditions in 
the `_confirm_futures` dictionary |\n\n## Root Cause\nThe code uses `id(future)`
as a dictionary key in `_confirm_futures`. While `id()` returns a unique 
identifier for an object's lifetime, Python can and does reuse memory addresses 
after objects are garbage collected. If:\n1. Future A is created with memory 
address X\n2. Future A completes and is garbage collected\n3. Future B is 
created and gets the same memory address X (memory reuse)\n4. A late callback 
for Future A could affect Future B\n\nThe current implementation attempts to 
mitigate this with a counter system, but there's still a race condition in the 
initialization logic.\n\n## Fix Plan\n1. Ensure `_confirm_counter` is always 
used instead of `id(future)` \n2. Make the counter increment atomic with proper 
locking\n3. Verify all code paths use the counter-based approach 
consistently\n4. Add a test that demonstrates the bug and verifies the fix\n\n##
Status: IN PROGRESS"})

